==Notes==
* The Elder Scrolls are retroactively the namesake of ''[[General:The Elder Scrolls|The Elder Scrolls]]'' franchise. When [[Arena:Arena|''Arena'']] transitioned from an action combat game into an RPG, "''The Elder Scrolls: Chapter I''" was added to the title, not only to better advertise that it was an RPG (which was a prerogative), but also to evoke a sort of tome like novel, something that new ''Chapters'' could be written into down the line. The team eventually picked "The Elder Scrolls", which took its name from Tamriel's mystical tomes of knowledge that told of its past, present, and future.{{ref|name=VijayInterview|group=UOL|1=[https://audioboom.com/posts/7102926-everyone-is-a-hero-of-their-own-story-with-vijay-lakshman The First Podcast: ''Everyone is a Hero of Their Own Story (with Vijay Lakshman)'']}}{{ref|name=10thAni|[[General:Elderscrolls.com Archive/The Elder Scrolls - 10th Anniversary|The Elder Scrolls - 10th Anniversary article]]}}  In ''Arena'', the Elder Scrolls were established as artifacts that could be used to locate lost locations. Certain messages would also speak of the Elder Scrolls foretelling the hero's adventure begins in the Imperial Dungeons, their fate will be written in the Elder Scrolls, and upon the conclusion of the main quest that the adventure "was but a chapter in the story of the Elder Scrolls". In ''Daggerfall'', it is further expanded that the Elder Scrolls have predicted major events.{{ref|name=DFUG|{{Cite Book|Daggerfall User's Guide|ns_base=Books}} - [[Books:Daggerfall User's Guide/History|History]]}} For many years the scrolls had only been mentioned and described, and the first physical appearance of an Elder Scroll in the games was in ''Oblivion''.{{Ref|name=TUH}}
* In the late 1990s, Bethesda had a plan to make a website with a lot of content about where they had "really" got the ''Elder Scrolls'' stories, involving fictional accounts of finding them in the three physical Elder Scroll replicas in the basement (two perfectly preserved, one broken, the latter of which was never used). The developers would get to "play" with the Scrolls and mull over the stories within, such as how they "found" the tale of Cyrus and "adapted" it into ''[[RG:Redguard|The Elder Scrolls Adventures: Redguard]]''. The developer diaries would contain "conversations" of how they would need to change the stories found within the Scrolls to make it them playable products, ultimately "compromising" to streamline the esoteric stories into easily digestible video games. It would have also given the developers a way of saying that they knew all future games were going to have discrepancies.{{Ref|name=mkirc|group=UOL}} The website ultimately never came to fruition, though [[General:Michael Kirkbride|Michael Kirkbride]] referenced this plan by jokingly saying all the Elder Scrolls seen in the games are copies of the "real" Elder Scrolls, found in the form of three giant cylinders kept in the vaults beneath [[Lore:White-Gold Tower|White-Gold Tower]].{{Ref|name=MKP|group=UOL}} <!--There remains replicas that have made public appearances: one in Bethesda's trophy case, one that makes convention rounds with ZOS (ie. E3 2011, ESO 10th anniversary event in Netherlands), and one kept in [[General:Emil Pagliarulo|Emil Pagliarulo]]'s house.-->
*[[Lore:Elder Alphabet|Runes]] written on the Elder Scrolls and their covers seem to be the same kind of runes that are found on the amulets of the Elder Council, which seem to be made of a metal similar to that of the Elder Scrolls' covers and crimped with a similar purple gem.
* The names of the Elder Scrolls that were fought over during the Three Banners War (Alma Ruma, Altadoon, Chim, Ghartok, Mnem, Ni-Mohk) are all [[Lore:Ehlnofex|Ehlnofex]] words mentioned in [[Lore:Mankar Camoran|Mankar Camoran]]'s ''[[Lore:Mythic Dawn Commentaries|Commentaries on the Mysterium Xarxes]]''.{{ref|name=MDC|{{Cite Book|Mythic Dawn Commentaries}}}}
* According to some legends, the [[Lore:Druids of Galen|Druids of Galen]] were in possession of an Elder Scroll, and followed its guidance to arrive to their new home on [[Lore:Y'ffelon|Y'ffelon]].{{ref|name=SHV1|{{Cite Book|Systres History: Volume 1}}}}
* A Moth Priest's interpretation of the contents of an Elder Scroll can be seen in [[Online:Terran's Notes|Terran's Notes]].
*It is said that prophecies revolving around [[Lore:Man|Man]] and [[Lore:Mer|Mer]] tend to be the most common among accounts in the Elder Scrolls but, according to the elders of the Cult of the Ancestor Moth, this was not always so. [[Lore:Sload|Sload]], [[Lore:Imga|Imga]], [[Lore:Faun|Fauns]], and [[Lore:Goblin|Goblins]] have all been referenced in the prophecies of the Scrolls.{{Ref|name=LAESMP}}

